--- !u!114 &1840613869
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 1840613868}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 6fc23b06f1c91bd4c847b7fc599d60ec, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::InfoObraMuseo
  nombreObjeto: Leonardo da Vinci aerial screw
  "textoES_Ni\xF1o": "\xA1El helic\xF3ptero que naci\xF3 en un dibujo!\nLeonardo
    da Vinci imagin\xF3 esta m\xE1quina que parec\xEDa un gran tornillo gigante de
    tela. Cre\xEDa que, si la girabas muy r\xE1pido, pod\xEDa subir por el aire como
    un helic\xF3ptero. Aunque nunca se construy\xF3 en su \xE9poca, \xA1fue una idea
    incre\xEDble adelantada a su tiempo!"
  "audioES_Ni\xF1o": {fileID: 0}
  textoES_Casual: "Una visi\xF3n temprana del vuelo vertical.\nLa \u201Ctornillo
    a\xE9reo\u201D de Leonardo fue dise\xF1ada alrededor de 1485. Era un concepto
    que buscaba elevarse girando una espiral de tela y madera, similar a un helic\xF3ptero
    moderno. Aunque no pod\xEDa volar realmente con los materiales de la \xE9poca,
    demuestra la sorprendente imaginaci\xF3n t\xE9cnica de Leonardo y su estudio
    del aire como un fluido."
  audioES_Casual: {fileID: 0}
  textoES_Experto: "Un antecedente conceptual del rotor helicoidal.\nLa Aerial Screw
    ilustra el inter\xE9s de Leonardo por las fuerzas de sustentaci\xF3n generadas
    por superficies helicoidales. Su dise\xF1o propone una estructura de ca\xF1as
    y lino recubierto en espiral, concebida para comprimir el aire y generar ascenso
    mediante rotaci\xF3n. Aunque f\xEDsicamente inviable debido a su peso, a la resistencia
    del material y a la ausencia de una fuente de potencia adecuada, el modelo es
    un hito te\xF3rico que anticipa el principio aerodin\xE1mico del rotor de helic\xF3ptero
    y el estudio del flujo helicoidal."
  audioES_Experto: {fileID: 0}
  "textoEN_Ni\xF1o": "The helicopter that was born from a drawing!\nLeonardo da Vinci
    imagined this machine shaped like a huge cloth screw. He believed that if you
    spun it fast enough, it could rise into the air like a helicopter. It wasn\u2019t
    built in his time, but it was an amazing idea far ahead of its era!"
  "audioEN_Ni\xF1o": {fileID: 0}
  textoEN_Casual: "An early vision of vertical flight.\nLeonardo\u2019s \u201Caerial
    screw,\u201D designed around 1485, attempted to rise by spinning a spiral of
    cloth and wood\u2014much like a modern helicopter. It couldn\u2019t truly fly
    with the materials available then, but it shows Leonardo\u2019s remarkable technical
    imagination and his belief that air could be manipulated like a fluid."
  audioEN_Casual: {fileID: 0}
  textoEN_Experto: "A conceptual precursor to the helicoidal rotor.\nThe Aerial Screw
    reflects Leonardo\u2019s interest in lift generated by helicoidal surfaces. Its
    design features a spiral structure of reed and linen intended to compress the
    air and produce upward force through rotation. Though impractical due to weight,
    material limits, and lack of a power source, it stands as a theoretical milestone
    foreshadowing helicopter rotor aerodynamics and helicoidal airflow studies."
  audioEN_Experto: {fileID: 0}
